## Context
This repository keeps being bitten by one defect, at three different levels,
and 0221 fixed it at only one of them. The shape: a gate's scope is a
hand-written list, so a thing REMOVED breaks the build loudly while a thing
ADDED is never looked at, and "all clear" is indistinguishable from "I never
looked".

Three instances in the single session that produced PR 52:

1. `spec/FIELD-REVIEW.md` — 114 kB of public prose — had been sitting outside
   `check_governance.py`'s `SCANNED` since it arrived. Found only because a
   different ticket went looking for something else.
2. `spec/TERMINOLOGY.md` landed on main *while* `check_chain_dedup.py` was
   being written, straight into that guard's brand-new hand-written list.
3. CLAUDE.md's own prose list of the guards named three when there were five.
   The Makefile had gained two that the sentence had not.

0221 closed the document axis for two guards, each with its own private
completeness function. What remains unguarded is the level above, and it is
the same defect wearing a different hat.

## The two gaps that remain

**The Makefile's guard list.** `make check` names its guards by hand. All five
`bench/check_*.py` are wired today — verified 1:1, no script without a target
and no target without a script. Nothing asserts it. A sixth guard whose author
forgets the `.PHONY` line and the `check:` dependency exists, is never run, and
`make check` exits 0. The guard would be dead on arrival and the build would
say nothing.

Partly, and only partly, mitigated by convention: four of the five guards'
suites assert `run(REPO) == 0` against the real tree, so bare pytest re-checks
the live repo even without Makefile wiring. That is a habit, not a structure,
and it held for four guards out of five — the terminology guard had no such
assertion until this ticket's own PR added one.

**`check_figures.py` has no completeness function at all.** Its `PROSE` dict is
hand-written and nothing discovers candidates. This one is not latent, it is
live: `spec/CONSTRAINTS.md` quotes measured figures and is not in `PROSE`, and
three `verification/*.md` files quote figures from outside it — one of them
says so about itself in its own text. That axis is ticket 0161's, blocked by
0160, and this ticket does not duplicate it. It is named here because it is the
third instance and it is what makes the class worth a standing test rather than
three separate repairs.

## Relevant files
- `Makefile` — the `check:` chain and the `.PHONY` line
- `bench/check_governance.py` — `uncovered()`, the fixed pattern
- `bench/check_chain_dedup.py` — `untriaged()`, the same pattern duplicated
- `tests/` — no test asserts any cross-cutting completeness property
- `tickets/0161-standing-guard-every-prose-doc-quoting-a.erg` — the figures axis
- `tickets/0221-the-governance-guard-s-scanned-set-is-ha.erg` — the document axis

## Actions
1. Add a test asserting the set of `bench/check_*.py` scripts equals the set
   invoked by the Makefile's `check` chain, so a guard cannot be added without
   being wired in.
2. Add a test asserting every guard's suite exercises the real repository, not
   only `tmp_path` fixtures — the convention that saved four guards should be a
   check rather than a habit.
3. Consider whether the two private completeness functions
   (`uncovered`, `untriaged`) want to become one shared helper. Weigh it against
   the cost of coupling the guards; two small duplicated functions may be the
   right answer, and "leave them" is an acceptable outcome of this action.

## Test
Red first, and the positive control is the whole point given what this ticket
is about. Write a fixture that adds a `bench/check_nothing.py` absent from the
Makefile and assert the test fails; write one whose suite has no real-repo
assertion and assert the test fails. A completeness test that has never been
seen to fail is the exact defect it was written against.

## Verification
- [x] Adding a guard script without Makefile wiring fails the suite, and the
      failure names the script
- [x] A guard suite that never touches the real repository fails, and the
      failure names the suite
- [x] Both new tests were observed red before being made green
- [x] `make check` passes

## Invariants
- No existing guard changes behaviour. This ticket adds tests about the gates,
  not new rules for the prose.
- 0161's and 0221's axes are not re-opened or duplicated here.

## Exit criteria
- A guard that is added but not wired into `make check` fails the suite, and a
  guard suite that never exercises the real repository fails the suite.
